02Preserve overlap; treat passwords as edgesHow can we guarantee coverage while typing fewer repeated characters? A baseline enumerates every n-digit password and concatenates them. For n=2,k=2, 00|01|10|11 uses 8 digits and guarantees coverage, but is not shortest. Enumeration and concatenation cost O(n·kⁿ) time and output space. Extra windows across block boundaries do not hurt coverage; the wasted opportunity is overlap.# Coverage baseline, not shortest / 覆盖基线，并非最短
from itertools import product

def concatenate_all(n, k):
    words = product(map(str, range(k)), repeat=n)
    return "".join("".join(word) for word in words)
How short can it be? A length-L string has at most L−n+1 full windows, each testing just one password. There are kⁿ passwords, so L ≥ kⁿ+n−1. Two-digit binary passwords need at least 4+2−1=5 digits; 00110 attains that bound. We now need a construction attaining it for every valid input.Keep only the last n−1 digits as a state, called a vertex. Appending digit d creates an n-digit password, called a directed edge; the new vertex is its final n−1 digits. From suffix 0, append 1: the password is 01 and the new suffix is 1. Below are all edges for n=2,k=2. Arrows mean state transitions caused by typing, not screen coordinates.Old suffixAppend digitPassword / edgeNew suffix00000010111010011111A tempting mistake is to stop at a visited vertex. Taking edge 00 from suffix 0 returns to 0, yet 01 is still unused! Stopping after 00 because the vertex repeats misses 01, 10, 11. Use each password edge once; suffix vertices may repeat.Why does such a route exist? Each suffix has k departures and k arrivals (prepend any digit). Appending the digits of any target suffix reaches it; for n=1 there is only the empty suffix. Thus the graph is connected and each vertex has equal arrival and departure counts. Following unused edges cannot get stuck away from the start: each arrival consumes an incoming edge and has a matching departure available. Return to the start to close a cycle. If unused edges remain, start another cycle at the appropriate vertex on the existing cycle and splice it in. A closed route using each edge once is an Euler circuit. Prefix the n−1 starting zeros, then add one digit per edge: exactly kⁿ+n−1 digits, hence optimal. This shortest coverage string is called a de Bruijn string.

05Map edges and finishing to PythonHow do these rules become an official submission? The source below implements Solution.crackSafe(n, k), returning a string. Python encodes suffixes as integers to avoid slicing and string hashing; the diagram displays those integers as zero-padded suffixes. The animation is a separate simulation and does not execute this source.Let V=k^(n−1), the number of suffix vertices. Encode vertex in base k. Appending digit makes vertex*k+digit the password-edge code; modulo V retains the last n−1 digits. For n=3,k=2, suffix 01 is code 1; append 1 to get 1*2+1=3, encoding 011. Then 3%4=3 encodes the new suffix 11. For n=1 or k=1, V=1 and every target is 0, while departure digits still distinguish the edges.next_digit[vertex] += 1 permanently consumes an edge. A vertex can be pushed repeatedly; no visited-vertex set is needed.stack.append((target, digit)) saves arrival information. stack.pop() happens only when departures are exhausted; incoming is not None excludes the initial entry.reversed(finished) yields forward edge digits. Prefix n−1 zeros to complete the first window. The iterative stack avoids recursion-depth risk from up to 4096 edges.E=kⁿ counts password edges; V=k^(n−1) counts suffixes. Each edge causes one push; each of E+1 entries pops once; cursors increment E times total. Initialization costs O(V), and reversal/join costs O(E+n). Total time and space are O(E+n), including cursors, stack, finished list and output, with bounded-size integer operations. Copied teaching snapshots use up to O(E²+En) storage for the small presets, separate from submission cost.Boundaries: n=1 has an empty prefix and returns each digit once. For k=1 the only password is n zeros and the shortest length is n. With n=4,k=8, E=4096 and the answer length is 4099. Empty passwords, k=0 and invalid graphs are outside the official contract. Answers are not unique: test coverage and minimum length, not equality with one example string.Copy Python sourceDownload